======

agent-remote-bridge is MIT licensed (see LICENSE).

This project reuses *design knowledge and protocol semantics* from the
following open-source projects. No source code is copied from them; the
implementations here are independent Python code written against the same
public HTTP protocol / hook JSON contracts.

1. Tencent/openclaw-weixin  (MIT)
   https://github.com/Tencent/openclaw-weixin
   Used for: the WeChat iLink Bot HTTP protocol (endpoint names, request/response
   field names, message_type/message_state/item_list encoding, errcode -14
   semantics). docs/RESEARCH.md records exactly which endpoints were verified.

2. Conn-Ho/wechat-claude-code  (Apache-2.0)
   https://github.com/Conn-Ho/wechat-claude-code
   Used for: independent cross-verification of the same iLink endpoints, and as
   prior art for combining agent hooks + an MCP server for approvals.

3. y49/tlive  (MIT)
   https://github.com/y49/tlive
   Used for: the architectural idea of riding on each vendor's own hook
   mechanism (a "notify" mode that can never block, and an opt-in "approve" mode
   that can hold a tool call), and the two-tier integration model
   (hooks-only vs. wrapped).

4. slopus/happy  (MIT)
   https://github.com/slopus/happy
   Used for: the general "local session <-> remote client" layering idea.

The optional terminal QR renderer depends on `qrcode` (BSD) and, for PNG
output, `Pillow` (HPND/MIT-CMU style). Both are optional; the bridge runs
without them and prints a scannable fallback payload instead.

